      <div>
        <h2 class="research-heading">Resolving Intent&ndash;Content Conflict in Retrieval</h2>
        <p class="research-lead">When someone asks for "a happy movie about war," the intent contradicts the content semantics. Most retrieval systems optimize for one signal or the other. This research asks whether you can resolve that conflict explicitly.</p>
        <p class="research-method"><strong>How:</strong> A heterogeneous knowledge graph over MovieLens 25M. User, Movie, and Emotion nodes — emotions as first-class entities with weighted EVOKES edges. A Graph Transformer encoder aligned to SentenceBERT space, with a tunable affective displacement penalty.</p>
        <p class="research-method"><strong>What we found:</strong> Edge-removal interventions confirmed emotion edges are causally necessary, not just correlated. The system learns when signals conflict and which to trust.</p>
        <p class="research-method" style="margin-bottom:0">Published at SAM International &middot; Ramapo College, 2025.</p>
      </div>
